    role="img"
    aria-label="Two timelines for one cache entry. The naive way, delete on every write: each write kills the entry and every refill is a 1,280-read recompute, so a busy bucket never benefits from the cache. What we do instead: a write shortens the entry to expire in 15 minutes, further writes during that window are free because the entry is already dying, the entry expires, and the next read refills it with the fresh size. A write storm costs one cache operation, and a stale size still cannot outlive 15 minutes."
    style={{ width: "100%", height: "auto" }}
  />
</MermaidFrame>

**Layer two: a per-pod debounce absorbs the storm.** A bucket taking 100 writes
per second would otherwise fire 100 cache operations per second. An in-memory
LRU keyed by bucket collapses that to roughly one operation per cooldown window,
per pod. The suppressed writes don't even pay a network round-trip. Cross-region
writes ride an event that already existed for the object cache. No new event
type, no producer change.

